---
import PageLayout from "../../layouts/PageLayout.astro";
import CardList from "../../components/CardList.astro";
import { topicLabels } from "../../lib/topics";
---

<PageLayout
  title="Modules"
  description="All LLMs Unplugged modules — the hands-on activities that lessons are built from."
>
  <h1>Modules</h1>

  <p>
    Modules are the building blocks of <em>LLMs Unplugged</em>&mdash;each one is
    a self-contained hands-on activity teaching one idea about how language
    models work. If you want a ready-to-run session built from these modules
    (with slides and timings), start from the <a href="/lessons/">lessons</a>
    instead.
  </p>

  <p>
    The modules split into <strong>Fundamentals</strong>&mdash;Training and
    Generation, which everyone should do&mdash;and <strong>Extensions</strong>
    you can mix and match based on interests and available time. New to <em
      >LLMs Unplugged</em
    >? Do <a href="/modules/training/">Training</a>, then
    <a href="/modules/generation/">Generation</a>, then pick whichever
    extensions catch your eye.
  </p>

  <p>
    The Fundamentals (and a couple of extensions) come in two flavours with a
    toggle at the top of the page; both teach the same core ideas, so pick
    whichever suits your group.
  </p>

  <p>
    The <strong>grid</strong> flavour uses pen, paper and dice. Students fill in a
    tally-mark table during training, then roll a d10 to sample from the counts during
    generation (a 60%/40% split between two candidate next words becomes &ldquo;roll
    a d10: 1-6 picks the first, 7-10 picks the second&rdquo;). It plugs straight into
    probability in the maths curriculum&mdash;if your group isn't comfortable with
    that style of sampling yet, run
    <a href="/modules/weighted-randomness/">Weighted Randomness</a> first as a warm-up.
  </p>

  <p>
    Each module card shows who it suits, how long it takes, what it runs on, and
    how road-tested it is: <em>workshop-tested</em> modules are the ones in our regularly
    delivered lessons, <em>piloted</em> ones have run in a room at least once, and
    <em>experimental</em> ones are written but haven't been run with a group yet&mdash;if
    you try one, we'd love to hear how it went.
  </p>

  <p>
    The <strong>cutouts</strong> flavour swaps dice for a spread of printed tokens.
    Students cut them out, lay them on a table, and play a colour-matching game to
    generate text&mdash;the maths happens automatically (more cutouts means more likely),
    so it works for any age. The cost is prep: someone has to generate, print and
    cut out the tokens beforehand (the
    <a href="/tools/#cutouts">Materials</a> page has ready-to-print packs).
  </p>

  <p>
    A third format, <strong>search sheets</strong>, skips both the training and
    the cutting: the whole text is dealt across the room, one sheet per person,
    and the room generates together by putting hands up. It's how we run the
    activity as a talk, or with a big class and thirty minutes&mdash;see
    <a href="/modules/search-sheets/">Search Sheets</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="fundamentals">Fundamentals</h2>
  <CardList topic="fundamentals" />

  <h2 id="extensions">Extensions</h2>
  <p>
    These can be done in any order after the Fundamentals. Each one explores a
    different aspect of how modern language models work.
  </p>

  <h3 id="scaling-up">{topicLabels["scaling-up"]}</h3>
  <CardList topic="scaling-up" />

  <h3 id="controlling-output">{topicLabels["controlling-output"]}</h3>
  <CardList topic="controlling-output" />

  <h3 id="how-models-understand">{topicLabels["how-models-understand"]}</h3>
  <p>
    How models use more than the previous word: a short-term memory that keeps
    text on topic, the induction trick behind in-context learning, and word
    embeddings for measuring meaning.
  </p>
  <CardList topic="how-models-understand" />

  <h3 id="adaptation-and-data">{topicLabels["adaptation-and-data"]}</h3>
  <CardList topic="adaptation-and-data" />
</PageLayout>
